1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,870
So we'll now speak to board effectiveness.

2
00:00:02,090 --> 00:00:04,530
I've described the duties and responsibilities of the

3
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:06,710
board but those are the basics, the very

4
00:00:06,710 --> 00:00:07,150
basics.

5
00:00:08,170 --> 00:00:10,530
A board has to be effective for it

6
00:00:10,530 --> 00:00:11,890
to add value to the business.

7
00:00:12,470 --> 00:00:14,230
So let's call board effectiveness, we'll take a

8
00:00:14,230 --> 00:00:18,130
short case study of how Facebook acquired Instagram.

9
00:00:19,630 --> 00:00:21,970
So Mark Zuckerberg owned at the point this

10
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:25,210
case study came up 28% of Facebook

11
00:00:25,210 --> 00:00:28,770
stock and controlled 57% of its voting

12
00:00:28,770 --> 00:00:29,230
rights.

13
00:00:30,070 --> 00:00:32,830
Facebook's public offering was only a few weeks

14
00:00:32,830 --> 00:00:33,230
away.

15
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:36,670
On the other hand, Kevin owned about 45

16
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:37,970
% of Instagram.

17
00:00:38,670 --> 00:00:40,410
The company at the time was 18 months

18
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:44,810
old, employed 13 people, had 35 million users,

19
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:46,030
nil revenue.

20
00:00:47,190 --> 00:00:50,790
On Thursday the 5th of April 2012, Zuckerberg

21
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:53,690
picked up his phone and asked Kevin for

22
00:00:53,690 --> 00:00:54,250
a meeting.

23
00:00:54,750 --> 00:00:56,870
Over the next three days, which happened to

24
00:00:56,870 --> 00:00:59,490
be over the weekend, the two CEOs negotiated

25
00:00:59,490 --> 00:01:01,790
in the privacy of Zuckerberg's home.

26
00:01:02,310 --> 00:01:06,190
On Sunday morning, April the 8th, Zuckerberg emailed

27
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:11,370
his board that he wanted to buy Instagram.

28
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:14,150
On Monday, the deal was announced.

29
00:01:15,030 --> 00:01:16,570
So a few things would have happened here,

30
00:01:17,490 --> 00:01:19,610
you know, like I said, or like the

31
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:23,110
case facts say, the public offering of Facebook

32
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:24,610
was just a few weeks away.

33
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:27,690
If the deal to buy Instagram had gone

34
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:30,670
wrong, it would have significantly affected the public

35
00:01:30,670 --> 00:01:31,130
offering.

36
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:35,870
Why did Zuckerberg not involve his board in

37
00:01:35,870 --> 00:01:36,590
this negotiation?

38
00:01:36,830 --> 00:01:38,970
A few reasons would come to mind.

39
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:41,510
One of which is the fact that Zuckerberg

40
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:44,550
seemingly had control of the board.

41
00:01:44,670 --> 00:01:47,170
He was the one that appointed almost all,

42
00:01:47,270 --> 00:01:48,790
in fact, all of the board members because

43
00:01:48,790 --> 00:01:51,070
essentially he owned the company.

44
00:01:51,270 --> 00:01:53,490
And so he appointed a few of his

45
00:01:53,490 --> 00:01:55,630
cronies as members of the board.

46
00:01:55,670 --> 00:01:58,170
So they didn't really have a say in

47
00:01:58,170 --> 00:02:00,090
the governing of the organisation.

48
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:04,250
Second thing, and this is very much true

49
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:06,950
of tech companies, he felt that the board

50
00:02:06,950 --> 00:02:10,390
might not have an understanding of the gravity

51
00:02:10,390 --> 00:02:11,850
of the opportunity.

52
00:02:12,510 --> 00:02:14,510
And he felt that he was slowed down

53
00:02:14,510 --> 00:02:16,250
or bogged down by the bureaucracy of holding

54
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:18,750
a board meeting and taking the trouble to

55
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:20,530
explain to the board.

56
00:02:21,070 --> 00:02:23,010
Some people have also said perhaps the board

57
00:02:23,010 --> 00:02:26,430
had given him a significant or delegated to

58
00:02:26,430 --> 00:02:29,610
him a significant portion of their authority.

59
00:02:30,270 --> 00:02:35,010
But regardless, acquisition, mergers and acquisitions, and a

60
00:02:35,010 --> 00:02:37,770
transaction of this nature should not or could

61
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:39,630
not have been delegated to the CEO.

62
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:42,930
Critically, the board should have considered the pros

63
00:02:42,930 --> 00:02:46,070
and cons of such a major acquisition and

64
00:02:46,070 --> 00:02:49,290
the impact of it on particularly the impending

65
00:02:49,290 --> 00:02:51,510
public offer of Facebook.

66
00:02:52,030 --> 00:02:55,230
Fortunately for Zuckerberg and the board and the

67
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:58,390
company as a whole, the deal has succeeded

68
00:02:58,390 --> 00:03:02,110
as we can see how profitable the addition

69
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:05,190
of Instagram to the business has been.

70
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:08,210
But this underscores the effectiveness of the board.

71
00:03:08,350 --> 00:03:11,270
How effective would one say that board was?

72
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:15,330
In my opinion, very ineffective because again, it

73
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:17,870
could have caused significant losses to the business.

74
00:03:18,110 --> 00:03:20,210
And so we go on to the definition

75
00:03:20,210 --> 00:03:23,870
of an effective board as described by the

76
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:25,730
Nigerian Code of Corporate Governance, which I will

77
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:25,990
read.

78
00:03:26,890 --> 00:03:29,530
It says a successful company is headed by

79
00:03:29,530 --> 00:03:33,610
an effective board which is responsible for providing

80
00:03:33,610 --> 00:03:38,130
entrepreneurial and strategic leadership, as well as promoting

81
00:03:38,130 --> 00:03:41,170
ethical, cultural and responsible corporate citizenship.

82
00:03:41,810 --> 00:03:44,770
As a link between stakeholders and the company,

83
00:03:45,310 --> 00:03:48,410
the board is to exercise oversight and control

84
00:03:48,410 --> 00:03:51,150
to ensure that management acts in the best

85
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:55,650
interest of shareholders and other stakeholders while sustaining

86
00:03:55,650 --> 00:03:57,770
the prosperity of the company.

87
00:03:58,390 --> 00:04:00,350
This is taken from principle one of the

88
00:04:00,350 --> 00:04:01,710
Nigerian Code of Corporate Governance.

89
00:04:01,830 --> 00:04:05,570
So essentially, beyond meeting the basics like we

90
00:04:05,570 --> 00:04:07,530
have described in the course of this session,

91
00:04:08,070 --> 00:04:10,010
the board also has to be effective.

92
00:04:10,610 --> 00:04:14,350
The board has to provide entrepreneurial and strategic

93
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:14,930
leadership.

94
00:04:15,790 --> 00:04:17,709
That's why many boards go to the trouble

95
00:04:17,709 --> 00:04:21,950
of identifying directors who can add value beyond

96
00:04:22,810 --> 00:04:26,890
just attending meetings and providing the regular oversight

97
00:04:26,890 --> 00:04:29,830
and they have to provide leadership to the

98
00:04:29,830 --> 00:04:30,130
business.

99
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:32,410
So broken down, the role of the board

100
00:04:32,410 --> 00:04:35,490
can be described as that of providing accountability.

101
00:04:36,270 --> 00:04:39,030
The board puts itself in a position where

102
00:04:39,030 --> 00:04:40,770
management is accountable to it.

103
00:04:41,350 --> 00:04:42,610
And one of the ways that the board

104
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:45,770
can be accountable to the board is what

105
00:04:45,770 --> 00:04:46,950
we had described earlier.

106
00:04:47,490 --> 00:04:50,550
The board sets key performance indicators and measures

107
00:04:50,550 --> 00:04:53,410
the performance of management against these key performance

108
00:04:53,410 --> 00:04:53,890
indicators.

109
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:56,630
So management knows that it is accountable to

110
00:04:56,630 --> 00:05:00,430
a body and not just accountable to itself.

111
00:05:00,850 --> 00:05:02,410
The board on the other hand is accountable

112
00:05:02,410 --> 00:05:08,430
to shareholders who have appointed ostensibly the directors.

113
00:05:09,210 --> 00:05:10,710
The board also provides leadership.

114
00:05:11,130 --> 00:05:14,110
Now this leadership is not academic.

115
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:17,690
The leadership is actually leadership that is responsible

116
00:05:17,690 --> 00:05:19,430
for strategy and direction.

117
00:05:20,090 --> 00:05:21,730
The board sets the tone in terms of

118
00:05:21,730 --> 00:05:25,390
where the business is headed and allows management

119
00:05:25,390 --> 00:05:29,470
to provide strategic initiatives to achieve the said

120
00:05:29,470 --> 00:05:29,930
strategy.

121
00:05:30,710 --> 00:05:32,350
The board is the one that sets the

122
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:35,430
ethical tone, what we describe as the tone

123
00:05:35,430 --> 00:05:35,970
at the top.

124
00:05:36,610 --> 00:05:38,610
And that tone is defined by the kind

125
00:05:38,610 --> 00:05:41,710
of people that we hire, how we evaluate

126
00:05:41,710 --> 00:05:46,270
performance and how we reward and punish bad

127
00:05:46,270 --> 00:05:46,730
behaviour.

128
00:05:47,510 --> 00:05:49,690
The board is also expected to measure and

129
00:05:49,690 --> 00:05:50,590
monitor performance.

130
00:05:50,770 --> 00:05:51,990
We have spoken about that earlier.

131
00:05:52,710 --> 00:05:55,350
The board is responsible for risk management, ensuring

132
00:05:55,350 --> 00:05:59,010
that the financial statements present a true and

133
00:05:59,010 --> 00:06:02,570
fair view and also managing succession planning and

134
00:06:02,570 --> 00:06:03,510
business continuity.

135
00:06:03,990 --> 00:06:06,070
We saw during COVID that a number of

136
00:06:06,070 --> 00:06:10,090
organisations did not survive COVID because they didn't

137
00:06:10,090 --> 00:06:14,010
have robust business continuity plans that would ensure

138
00:06:14,010 --> 00:06:16,370
that whatever the crisis, whatever the nature of

139
00:06:16,370 --> 00:06:18,870
the crisis, the organisation is able to go

140
00:06:18,870 --> 00:06:19,330
beyond.

141
00:06:19,930 --> 00:06:22,670
That also affects succession planning.

142
00:06:22,670 --> 00:06:24,550
The board should not leave the issue of

143
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:27,750
succession planning at critical levels in the organisation

144
00:06:27,750 --> 00:06:29,450
to management.
